  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 PM EDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Well-advertised rainy and increasingly \nwindy conditions on tap over the next 24-36 hours with current focus \non two inbound areas of low pressure. The first currently sitting \nnear the Manitoba/Ontario/Minnesota border and dropping southeast, \nwhile the second wave continues to trek northeast out of the south-\ncentral Plains. The parent waves of these features currently in the \nprocess of phasing upstream with the end result expected to be one \nlarger area of sfc low pressure that crosses lower Michigan tonight \nbefore racing quickly east to New England through the day on Friday. \nUpper-level heights build behind this system over the weekend with \nrenewed surface high pressure looking to renew drier conditions and \ngradually moderating temperatures into at least the middle of next \nweek. \n\nForecast Details: Primary focus through Friday evening revolves \naround incoming widespread rain this afternoon through much of \ntonight. As this system pushes downstream on Friday, continued wrap \naround moisture will result in additional numerous showers and \nincreasingly strong NNW winds. Strong cold air advection on Friday \nas a result, which will eventually bring an increased lake component \n(aiding in additional shower activity), especially Friday afternoon \nthrough Friday evening.  PWs remain progged as high as 1-1.5\" across \nthe forecast area early this evening through the first half of the \novernight hours -- some +1 to 3 SD above the climatological mean for \nmid-October. Should be a fairly steady moderate rain for much of the \nforecast area through tonight, especially beneficial for those in \nexpanding drought across the eastern U.P. Favored QPF amounts this \nafternoon through Friday afternoon up a bit from 24 hours ago -- \nlargely ranging from 0.60 - 1.00\". As one would expect, \nprobabilities have also increased for localized amounts of 1.00\"+, \nup to 50-80% across portions of the area, most notably in NNW flow \nlake effect area that see the most numerous continued shower \nactivity Friday afternoon/evening.  Some very low (<15%) probs for \n2\"+ amounts still evident over the same area. \n\nDepending on how much shower activity remains after sunset Friday, \nwhich there will likely be at least some, a few inland/higher \nterrain locations could mix in or change over to some wet snow given \ncontinued cold advection with H8 temps progged as low as -6 to -8C. \nThink the primary limiting factors to this will be decreasing \nmoisture depth with the loss of synoptic support and low-level winds \nalmost out of the due north by this point, which will likely limit \noverall shower coverage across those typically colder higher terrain \nareas up the spine of north-central lower. Nonetheless, it's still \nwithin the realm of possibilities that a few spots coat the ground \nin the season's first snow.\n\nWinds Friday become increasingly gusty out of the north-northwest. \nFrequent gusts expected to range from 25-35 mph area-wide with those \nnearest the coasts having the highest likelihood to gust 40+ mph at \ntimes Friday afternoon. \n\nThis weekend, drier weather expected to prevail with continued cool \ntemperatures on Saturday (low-mid 50s) moderating for Sunday (low-\nmid 60s).  That said, and while high pressure will largely be in \ncontrol locally, low pressure passing well to north through Canada \nwill result in enough of a pinched pressure gradient to bring the \nreturn of breezy to gusty winds Saturday afternoon through Sunday \nnight. High temps Monday - Wednesday largely in the 60s with the \nnext chance for rain returning toward Thursday of next week.",
